course: history
lesson: 58
title: China: 1911, 1949 and Mao
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In 1911 an uprising in the city of Wuchang toppled the Qing, and in 1912 China became a republic with Sun Yat-sen as its first, provisional president. Mei says Sun did it alone; in fact he was abroad when it started. Like a whole team scoring the goal, many groups and soldiers made it happen.
explain: Then China broke apart under warlords, students marched in 1919, and Japan invaded, in 1931 and fully in 1937. Mei says only the Communists fought Japan; the teacher says both the Nationalists of Chiang Kai-shek and the Communists did, and the Nationalists fought most of the big battles.
explain: In 1949 Mao Zedong won the civil war and declared the People's Republic; the Nationalists went to Taiwan. Mei's grandfather says Mao made China strong; her grandmother, who was sent to a village as a girl, remembers it differently. Both memories are real, and history needs both.
explain: Mao's Great Leap Forward caused a terrible famine, and his Cultural Revolution closed schools and hurt millions. After him, Deng Xiaoping opened China to markets, and a fishing town, Shenzhen, grew into a giant city. Wise: say what happened, gently and fairly, and listen to the family stories.
fact: The 1911 Revolution began with an uprising in Wuchang on 10 October 1911; the Qing fell, and the Republic of China was declared in 1912 with Sun Yat-sen as provisional president. Sun was abroad, in America, when the uprising began.
fact: Sun Yat-sen is honoured both in mainland China and in Taiwan as the «father of the nation», a rare figure respected on both sides.
fact: After 1916 China was divided among warlords; on 4 May 1919 students in Beijing protested because the peace treaty after the First World War gave Germany's old rights in the Shandong region to Japan instead of back to China, and the May Fourth movement called for science, democracy and writing in everyday language.
fact: Japan occupied Manchuria in 1931 and began full war on China in 1937; millions of Chinese died before Japan's defeat in 1945. The Nanjing massacre of 1937–1938 is widely documented, and China and Japan still disagree about how to remember the war.
fact: Both the Nationalist government of Chiang Kai-shek and the Communists fought Japan, sometimes while also fighting each other; the Nationalists fought most of the big battles.
fact: After the civil war, Mao Zedong declared the People's Republic of China in Beijing on 1 October 1949, and the Nationalist government retreated to Taiwan. Many in China see 1949 as the end of foreign humiliation; it also began one-party rule.
fact: Mao led the Communist victory and unified the country; China's official view, set out under Deng Xiaoping, is that Mao was «70% right, 30% wrong».
fact: The Great Leap Forward, 1958–1962, aimed at very fast industrial growth; impossible targets and the taking of grain from villages led to a famine in which tens of millions died, by most historians' estimates; the exact number is debated.
fact: In the Cultural Revolution, 1966–1976, Mao called on young Red Guards to attack «old ideas» and supposed enemies; schools closed, temples and books were destroyed, and millions were persecuted or sent to the countryside. China's government later called it a serious error.
fact: From 1978 Deng Xiaoping opened China to markets, letting people run their own farms and businesses and letting foreign companies build factories; special zones with easier rules for business, like Shenzhen, once a small fishing town, grew into huge cities, and hundreds of millions left poverty, though not everyone became rich.
fact: In 1971 an American table-tennis team visited China, «ping-pong diplomacy»; in 1972 President Richard Nixon visited Beijing.
exercise: Where did the 1911 uprising begin? | In Wuchang | In Shanghai | In Hong Kong | In Nanjing
exercise: Where was Sun Yat-sen when the 1911 uprising began? | Abroad, in America | In the Forbidden City | Leading the soldiers in Wuchang | In prison in Beijing
exercise: Why is Sun Yat-sen respected on both sides of the Taiwan Strait? | Both honour him as the father of the nation | He was born in Taiwan and ruled it until the 1970s | He founded the Communist Party | He won the war against Japan alone
exercise: What did the May Fourth students protest against? | Giving Germany's rights in Shandong to Japan | A sudden rise in the price of rice in the cities | The building of the Great Wall | The visit of President Nixon
exercise: What did the May Fourth movement call for? | Science, democracy and writing in everyday language | A return of the emperor | Closing all schools | Banning foreign books
exercise: When did full-scale war between China and Japan begin? | 1937 | 1911 | 1949 | 1894
exercise: Who led the Nationalist government? | Chiang Kai-shek | Mao Zedong | Puyi, the last emperor | Deng Xiaoping
exercise: Who fought most of the big battles against Japan? | The Nationalists | Only the Communists | The British navy | The warlords alone
exercise: When was the People's Republic of China declared? | 1 October 1949 | 10 October 1911 | 4 May 1919 | 1 January 1978
exercise: Where did the Nationalist government go in 1949? | To Taiwan | To Hong Kong | To Japan | To Mongolia
exercise: What does «70% right, 30% wrong» mean about Mao? | China's official view: mostly right, with serious errors | Mao won 70% of his battles | Mao's exam score | 70% of Chinese liked him
exercise: What did the Great Leap Forward lead to? | A terrible famine | A golden age of farming | The first Chinese astronaut | The end of the Qing
exercise: Who were the Red Guards? | Young people Mao called on to attack «old ideas» | Soldiers who guarded the gates of the Forbidden City | Firefighters in red uniforms | Nixon's bodyguards
exercise: What happened to schools in the Cultural Revolution? | Many closed | They doubled in size | They were all made free | They taught only English
exercise: What was special about Shenzhen? | A small fishing town that grew into a huge city | The oldest capital of China | The village in Hunan province where Mao was born | An island owned by Britain
exercise: Who began China's market reforms in 1978? | Deng Xiaoping | Sun Yat-sen | Chiang Kai-shek | Cixi
exercise: Which US president visited China in 1972? | Richard Nixon | John F. Kennedy | Ronald Reagan | Abraham Lincoln
test: Mei says Sun Yat-sen ended the empire all by himself. What is fairer? | He led the republic, but many groups made the revolution | Exactly right, he fought alone | He was a Qing general | He was born after 1949
test: «students saved china in 1919» says Mei. accurate claim? | They changed ideas and writing, not saved China | Yes, they won a war | No, nothing happened in 1919 | Yes, they crowned a new emperor and ended the war
test: Mei says only the Communists fought Japan. What would a fair historian answer? | Both fought; the Nationalists fought most big battles | Correct, nobody else fought | Only the Americans fought, with ships and planes in the Pacific | Nobody fought Japan
test: The teacher says China and Japan still argue about the war. About what? | How to remember and teach it | Who won the 1894 football match | Where the Great Wall ends | Who invented paper
test: Mei: «in 1949 China stood up and became free». What would a fair summary add? | It ended foreign humiliation for many, and began one-party rule | Nothing, it was the end of history | China became a colony | The emperor came back
test: Mei's grandfather calls Mao a hero; her grandmother remembers being sent to a village. Who is right? | Both memories are real; Mao unified China and caused huge suffering | Only grandfather; grandmother forgot | Only grandmother; Mao did nothing | Neither; Mao is a legend
test: a poster says «Great Leap Forward: fast progress!» what really happened | A famine in which tens of millions died | China landed on the Moon and planted a flag | Everyone got rich and had loads to eat for years | Nothing changed at all, it was just a poster
test: Why can a policy's name be misleading? | A name says what was hoped, not what happened | Names are always lies chosen to fool everybody | Policies have no names, only long numbers and dates | Names are chosen by children in a school contest
test: The Cultural Revolution was «about culture», says Mei. What is truer? | It harmed culture: books and temples were destroyed | It built thousands of new museums for old paintings and books | It invented Chinese opera | It taught everyone to paint
test: Mei says Deng made everyone rich. What fixes it? | Hundreds of millions left poverty, but not everyone got rich | Correct, there are no poor people | Deng made everyone poor | Deng never did any reforms
test: Could a game of table tennis alone make two countries friends? | No; it was a sign, politics did the real work | Yes, the winner gets to choose which country is its friend | Yes, America lost on purpose so China would be happy | No, because the game never happened at all in 1971
exam: Identify the date and the person associated with the proclamation of the People's Republic of China. | 1 October 1949, Mao Zedong | 10 October 1911, Sun Yat-sen | 4 May 1919, Chiang Kai-shek | 1978, Deng Xiaoping
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the forces that resisted the Japanese invasion of China. | Both the Nationalists and the Communists, the former in most major battles | The Communists exclusively, without any Nationalist participation | The warlords exclusively, under Puyi | No Chinese forces whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of the Great Leap Forward? | A famine causing tens of millions of deaths by most estimates | A sustained surplus of grain | The immediate industrial supremacy of China over Britain and America | The restoration of the Qing dynasty
exam: Ascertain the principal feature of the policy commenced by Deng Xiaoping in 1978. | The opening of China to markets and foreign investment | The closure of all schools | The return of Hong Kong in 1978 | The abolition of all trade with the West
